{"name":"Southwest Stone","hex":"#de9f85","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"southwest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The intercardinal compass point halfway between south and west; specifically at a bearing of 225°."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, in or pertaining to the southwest; southwestern.","Situated toward or in the direction of the southwest; southwestward; southwesterly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/southwest"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Points of the compass","blocks":[{"text":"The points of the compass are a set of horizontal, radially arrayed compass directions used in navigation and cartography. A compass rose is primarily composed of four cardinal directions—north, east, south, and west—each separated by 90 degrees, and secondarily divided by four ordinal (intercardinal) directions—northeast, southeast, southwest, and northwest—each located halfway between two cardinal directions. Some disciplines such as meteorology and navigation further divide the compass with additional azimuths. Within European tradition, a fully defined compass has 32 \"points\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Compass-rose-32-pt.svg/330px-Compass-rose-32-pt.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Points_of_the_compass"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Bowditch1916p15CompassRose.png/500px-Bowditch1916p15CompassRose.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Bowditch1916p15CompassRose.png/1280px-Bowditch1916p15CompassRose.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Bowditch1916p15CompassRose.png/500px-Bowditch1916p15CompassRose.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Compass rose from American Practical Navigator, 1916","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABowditch1916p15CompassRose.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ming-marine-compass.jpg/330px-Ming-marine-compass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ming-marine-compass.jpg/330px-Ming-marine-compass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ming dynasty 24-pointed compass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMing-marine-compass.jpg"},{"srcset":"h